            <ArticleTitle>Short-term (28 days) prognosis between genders according to the type of coronary event (Q-wave versus non-Q-wave acute myocardial infarction versus unstable angina pectoris).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1161-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The type of acute coronary syndrome may account for different prognoses between men and women after myocardial infarction. This study assessed gender differences in 28-day mortality rates for first or recurrent Q-wave and non-Q-wave myocardial infarctions and unstable angina by using data from 5 registries that included 20,836 patients (24.8% women). Mortality rates were higher in women with first Q-wave myocardial infarction but not in the other patients after adjusting for confounding variables.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
